### Description

Angular SSR can exhaust the Node.js heap while inlining critical CSS when server-rendered HTML contains deeply nested elements that match descendant selectors from the application's stylesheet.

This can happen with a normal sanitized `[innerHTML]` binding.


### Minimal Reproduction


For example:

```ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `<article class="rich-text" [innerHTML]="content"></article>`,
})
export class App {
  readonly content = '<ul>'.repeat(674);
}
```

The application stylesheet only needs normal descendant selectors. The reproduction uses nested-list rules similar to:

```scss
$list-nesting-depth: 8;

@function nested-lists($levels) {
  $selector: '';

  @for $i from 1 through $levels {
    $selector: '#{$selector} ul';
  }

  @return $selector;
}

@for $level from 1 through $list-nesting-depth {
  .rich-text#{nested-lists($level)} li {
    margin-top: 0.2em;

    p {
      margin: 0 0 0.4em;
    }

    code {
      padding: 0.15em 0.4em;
    }

    blockquote {
      padding-left: 1em;
    }

    .mention {
      font-weight: 600;
    }

    .badge {
      padding: 0 0.6em;
    }

    .footnote-ref {
      vertical-align: super;
    }

    .task-list-item {
      list-style-type: none;
    }
  }
}
```

With `optimization.styles.inlineCritical` enabled, a single request containing **2,696 bytes** of nested `<ul>` markup consistently terminates a worker using a 512 MiB V8 heap:

```text
FATAL ERROR: Ineffective mark-compacts near heap limit
Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory
```

An equal-size control only changes the tag:

```text
candidate: '<ul>'.repeat(674)
control:   '<ol>'.repeat(674)
```

Both inputs have the same nesting depth, element count, payload size and Angular sanitization path.

